-- Conformance: a special-cased built-in name is reserved in the CONSTRUCTOR
-- namespace too (Chapter 8, Section 8.4.1)
-- Tests: E158 — a constructor named `Tuple` is rejected at check (#1397).
-- The third of the E158 fixtures. Its siblings reserve the `data` TYPE
-- name (ch08_builtin_adt_redefinition_rejected for `Future`,
-- ch08_builtin_tuple_redefinition_rejected for `Tuple`); this one reserves
-- the constructor name, which code generation keys separately: constructor
-- layouts are flattened by name across every ADT, so this declaration's
-- fixed layout displaced the built-in carrier's and silently disabled the
-- `@Nat` -> `@Int` widening guard on a genuine built-in tuple construction
-- elsewhere in the same program. A `Bool` payload is deliberate: an `Int`
-- one re-guards the component by coincidence and hides the hole.
private data ZzBox {
  Tuple(Bool)
}

public fn main(-> @Int)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  0
}
